:name: CubeRange CSP ping
:description: Two CubeRange nodes exchanging CSP (libcsp v2.1) over a shared CAN hub

# Execution profile (design 4.3; rules G1/G2 of section 9.2). Renode's Monitor has no conditionals,
# so the profile is a file the harness swaps by overriding $profile - the same mechanism $injector
# uses, and for the same reason: a relative include inside an included file resolves against
# Renode's own working directory, not this file. Measured: `include @sub/inner.resc` from a script
# elsewhere reports "File does not exist: sub/inner.resc".
#
# This include is FIRST on purpose. A failing command aborts the rest of a .resc file (measured:
# the line after a bad include never runs), so a profile that cannot be found takes the machines
# down with it and the failure is loud. Last, it would have been silent in the worst possible way:
# the scenario would build every node and run with NO quantum set, falling back to Renode's 100 us
# default, which changes firmware timing without any error.
#
# Emulation-scope settings before any machine exists are fine - measured.
#
# The default below only serves someone running this by hand from a clone in $HOME; every harness
# passes an absolute path via src/cuberange/renode/profile.py.
$profile?=@~/space-cs-sim/scripts/profiles/interactive.resc
include $profile

# Build the two images first:
#   make firmware
# then run:
#   $RENODE_DIR/renode --console -e "include @scripts/multi-node/csp_ping.resc"

# /tmp/zcsp1 and /tmp/zcsp2 were the defaults here, and nothing has built into those directories
# for a long time - `make demo` passes the real paths, so the stale values never surfaced. Derived
# from $out now, like every other scenario.
$n1?=$out/build-n1/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$n2?=$out/build-n2/zephyr/zephyr.elf
# Renode's Monitor cannot concatenate a variable with a literal, so each backend path
# is its own variable. `$out/n1.uart` fails with "Could not tokenize here".
# Every path below hangs off $out, and $out has NO default on purpose.
#
# These lines used to read @/tmp/cuberange/build-comm/zephyr/zephyr.elf and so on - one absolute
# path shared by every checkout of this repository. A second working copy on the same machine
# built into it, and this scenario then loaded THAT checkout's firmware without a word. The
# exercises made it worse by overriding only the one image under test, so the node being attacked
# came from here and the nodes around it came from somebody else.
#
# Unset, $out resolves to nothing and LoadELF fails where you can see it. That is the intended
# behaviour: refusing to start beats starting with the wrong firmware. The harness always passes
# it; for a manual run, so must you:
#
#   renode -e '$out=@'"$(make -s out)" -e 'include @<this file>' -e start
$n1uart?=$out/n1.uart
$n2uart?=$out/n2.uart

emulation CreateCANHub "canHub"

mach create "N1"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub
sysbus LoadELF $n1
# usart3 is Zephyr's console on this board. The space link must NOT share it - see the design's
# UART assignment section; a client connected to a console-sharing link socket receives 9239 bytes
# of boot banner before any application byte.
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $n1uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "N2"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub
sysbus LoadELF $n2
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $n2uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

# Renode's defaults cost roughly 8x: a 100 us quantum plus a real-time throttle that caps the
# emulation at exactly 1.0x. Measured on four nodes: 0.301x default, 1.000x with the quantum
# alone, 2.34x with both. 2 ms is the largest quantum that keeps firmware output byte-identical
# to a 100 us reference - above it, timing drifts silently and only shows up in longer runs.
